---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../Footer.astro';
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslations, useTranslatedPath } from '../../../i18n/utils';

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const t = useTranslations(lang);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);

const posts = [
  { href: '/qibla-finder/', title: 'How to Find Qibla Direction Anywhere', date: 'October 4, 2026', desc: 'Find the direction of the Kaaba from any location with compass and location integration.' },
];

const KWS =
  'online qibla, qibla direction, qibla direction arrow, live gibla direction online, compass qibla, find qibla direction, quick qibla direction, where is qibla direction, qibla direction compass degrees, qibla finder, qibla direction compass, online qibla direction, online qibla finder, online qibla compass, online qibla location';

const breadcrumbLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://instanten.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Online Qibla', item: 'https://instanten.com/qibla-finder/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://instanten.com/qibla-finder/blog/' },
  ],
};

---
<Layout
  title="Online Qibla Blog — Qibla Direction Guides"
  description="Guides for the online Qibla: how the Qibla direction is measured, compass accuracy tips, and how to set out a prayer space wherever you are."
  keywords={KWS}
  ogImage="/og-image.png"
  ogImageAlt="Online Qibla blog"
  breadcrumbLd={breadcrumbLd}>
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-[48rem] px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
    <a href={translatePath('/qibla-finder/')} class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-body hover:text-ink transition-colors mb-8">← Back to Qibla Finder</a>
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-2">Blog</h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono mb-4">Last updated: November 2026</p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Practical guides for the <strong>online Qibla</strong>: how the bearing is worked out from your own
      coordinates, why a phone compass drifts near metal, and how to set out a prayer space in a room you
      have never used before.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A <strong>qibla direction compass</strong> reading is only as good as the sensor behind it, so the
      notes here cover calibration, interference and the difference between a magnetic heading and a
      true bearing. There is also a walkthrough for the traveller's case — a
      <strong>quick Qibla direction</strong> check in a hotel room with no marker on the wall — and an
      explainer for the <strong>qibla direction arrow</strong> and what it is actually pointing at.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      If you are standing in a room right now, the short answer is on the main page: allow location, read
      the <strong>qibla direction compass degrees</strong> value, then turn until the arrow lines up. The
      notes below are the long answer — why a <strong>compass qibla</strong> reading drifts, why a
      <em>where is qibla direction</em> search gives five different answers, and how to get a steady
      <strong>online Qibla compass</strong> reading in a building full of steel.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Two more cases get their own notes: the traveller's <strong>online Qibla location</strong> check on
      landing in a new city, and the car or train where a
      <strong>live gibla direction online</strong> reading keeps moving because the vehicle does. Keep an
      <strong>online Qibla finder</strong> tab open and both become a two-second glance.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-10">
      Every guide uses the same free <strong>Qibla finder</strong> above, so you can read a step and try
      it straight away. If a <strong>Qibla direction</strong> reading looks wrong, start with the accuracy
      notes — nine times out of ten it is interference, not the calculation. If you only need one line: an
      <strong>online Qibla direction</strong> reading is right to a couple of degrees when the phone is
      calibrated and away from metal.
    </p>

    <ul class="space-y-8">
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li class="border-b border-hairline pb-6">
          <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono mb-2">{post.date}</p>
          <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-2">
            <a href={translatePath(post.href)} class="hover:text-link transition-colors">{post.title}</a>
          </h2>
          <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed">{post.desc}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>